{
  "bioavailability_prediction": "high",
  "confidence": "moderate",
  "revision_action": "keep",
  "supportive_card_ids": ["C01","C10","C25","C30","C09","C26","C44","C45","C46","C48","C49","C55","C63","C57","C56","C58"],
  "contradictory_card_ids": ["C20","C21"],
  "prediction_basis_card_ids": ["C01","C10","C09","C26","C44","C45","C49","C55","C63","C57"],
  "claims": [
    {
      "claim": "The query is carprofen, a carbazole propionic acid with an aryl chloride and carboxylic acid. The closest transferable context, tolfenamic acid (an aryl-chloride carboxylic-acid NSAID), showed oral bioavailability of 91% (C01) and about 100% (C10) in cirrhosis patients, with L2 values of 113% (C25) and 126% (C30), all affirmatively above the 20% threshold.",
      "card_ids": ["C01","C10","C25","C30"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "2,4-D, a small aryl-chloride carboxylic acid, showed rapid and complete oral bioavailability across species (C09) and virtually 100% systemic availability in rats (C26), supporting high F for this acidic aryl-chloride class.",
      "card_ids": ["C09","C26"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L4 fraction-absorbed records for related acidic NSAIDs show complete or near-complete intestinal absorption: indomethacin 100% (C44), naproxen 99-100% (C45, C49, C55), fenoprofen readily absorbed (C48), and AZD3582 at least 94% (C46).",
      "card_ids": ["C44","C45","C46","C48","C49","C55"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L5 Caco-2 records show high permeability and low efflux for several chloroaromatic and acidic compounds (e.g., C63 efflux ratio 1; C57 efflux ratio 1.09; C56 efflux ratio 0.97; C58 efflux ratio 2.4), indicating that efflux is unlikely to be a major barrier for this high-permeability class; however, none of these records is for carprofen and no direct intestinal-metabolism measurement for the query is supplied.",
      "card_ids": ["C63","C57","C56","C58"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "The low-F records (indacaterol in CD-1 mice 1%, C20; rac-KTP in horses 5.75%, C21) are species- and formulation-specific and do not transfer to the query; no affirmative threshold-relevant evidence supports F < 20%.",
      "card_ids": ["C20","C21"]
    }
  ],
  "new_evidence_assessment": [
    {
      "family": "gut_wall_efflux_intestinal_metabolism",
      "applicability": "moderate",
      "direction": "supportive",
      "decision_effect": "no_change",
      "card_ids": ["C56","C57","C58","C59","C60","C61","C62","C63","C64","C65","C66","C67"]
    }
  ],
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct absolute oral bioavailability measurement for carprofen itself is present in the supplied records.",
    "The closest structural context, tolfenamic acid, was studied under cirrhosis, which can alter first-pass and enterohepatic disposition; the query has no reported external condition.",
    "L4 fraction-absorbed and L5 Caco-2 records are not systemic F measurements; first-pass hepatic metabolism and clearance remain unquantified for the query.",
    "L3 oral exposure studies lack IV comparators, so they do not directly quantify absolute F.",
    "L5 Caco-2 records are for other molecules, not carprofen, and no intestinal metabolism assay for the query is supplied.",
    "Ionization at pH 7.4 (pKa 4.33) and possible pH-dependent solubility/permeability remain unquantified."
  ],
  "decision_summary": "Prediction remains high. L5 adds only indirect Caco-2 permeability/efflux evidence for other compounds; it is supportive of high permeability and low efflux but does not directly measure carprofen F or intestinal metabolism. High-F aryl-chloride carboxylic-acid contexts (tolfenamic acid, 2,4-D) and complete fraction-absorbed records for acidic NSAIDs continue to support F >= 20%. Confidence remains moderate."
}

